ich restricts blood flow to the lungs, leading to cyanosis. Right ventricular hypertrophy is a result of the increased workload due to the narrowed pulmonary outflow tract. The ventricular septal defect allows oxygenated and deoxygenated blood to mix, contributing to cyanosis. The aorta overriding the ventricular septal defect means it receives blood from both ventricles.\n\nAmong the provided options, the correct statement is that normal hemoglobin in patients with TOF does not rule out iron deficiency anemia. This is because patients with TOF can have chronic hypoxemia leading to anemia, and iron deficiency can still occur even if hemoglobin levels are normal. Other statements are incorrect: the tricuspid valve is not the most common valve affected by bacterial endocarditis in TOF, cerebral arterial thrombosis is less common than cerebral venous thrombosis, and refractory heart failure is not a common complication of TOF. The most common age of presentation for brain abscess is not between 6 and 12 months. \n"], "raw_output": "Option: B"}
